+++
title: Followership
hook: How do you handle not being in charge?
hook2: Do you wait to be directed, or do you find ways to make the environment better for everyone?
+++

Almost everyone spends more of their life following than leading on a team, in a family, in a community, in a partnership. Yet almost no one is taught how to do it well, and very few ever consciously embrace this role.

We are a deeply social species, constantly finding ourselves in tribes and groups that require us to step back and move forward with others. While leadership gets all the books, workshops, and cultural applause, most of us don’t actually want the burden of constant decision-making. We’re left to wonder if our contribution matters just as much as the person at the front. This is where the deliberate practice of followership comes in. Good followership is not passivity, and it is certainly not a waiting room for leadership (Zoller & Uhl-Bien, 2010). 

Followership is the active, willing, and critical behavior of team members who accept guidance to help achieve shared goals. It is the deliberate choice to place the group’s success above your ego. It is not empty; it is a choice. When you let go of the lead, you gain a unique perspective. Your mind is free to focus on the how rather than the what, becoming the eyes, the hands, and the heart of the endeavor. While the leader charts the course, you are checking the compass and watching the weather. There is a quiet freedom in this. No more guessing whether your idea was “good enough.” No more shouldering the weight of the outcome alone. Just the pure, almost meditative work of showing up fully for what’s ahead. You stop chasing the spotlight and start mastering the craft of collaboration.

It is about the craft of supporting a direction you didn’t set: how to commit without disappearing, how to disagree without undermining, and how to tell the difference between loyalty and compliance (Kelman, 1958). What do you owe the person leading, and what do they owe you? When is speaking up the most supportive thing you can do, and when is it just noise? How do you stay engaged with a decision you actually argued against? Sometimes we follow poorly, we become too passive and wait to be told what to do, or maybe we become too critical and pull at the fabric of the team. Coaching helps you understand your natural style. We work together to uncover where you find joy in execution and where you might be holding back energy because you’re waiting for permission to step forward.

There is also the question of alignment. Ask yourself: When was the last time you followed someone’s lead and felt genuinely at peace with it? How do you feel when you hand over the reins, do you feel a loss of control, or do you feel relief? These are not questions of hierarchy; they are questions of fit. How do you choose who is worth following in the first place, and how do you know when it’s time to stop? When you are the follower, you are the one who notices the subtle shift in the room, the detail in the work, the connection in the eyes. You are the one who makes the vision real. Followership allows us to rest from the burden of leadership while focusing on execution, connection, and the nuances that get missed when staring too far at the horizon.

It’s deeply practical, too. Whether you’re supporting a client through a transition, following a coach into a new practice, or simply navigating a team that thrives on mutual trust, followership becomes a strength. It’s not about yielding; it’s about trusting the rhythm of the group. A great follower amplifies the leader and finds joy in the execution of the vision. You don’t need to be the loudest voice in the room to shift its energy. You just need to know how to listen, how to step in, and how to trust the current. 

Good followership is not passivity, and it is not a waiting room for leadership. It is its own mindset and skill, practiced deliberately. Those who are best at it make everyone around them, including whoever is in charge, better.

We invite you to explore followership not as a stepping stone to leadership, but as a complete and powerful way of being in the world. If you’ve ever wondered whether your ability to follow is actually your superpower, let’s discover how to ride the wave together.